`),sampleAnswer:["To turn the mixture pink, the equilibrium must shift to the **left** so that $[\\mathrm{Co(H_2O)_6}^{2+}]$ increases and $[\\mathrm{CoCl_4}^{2-}]$ decreases.","","**Change 1 — Cool the test tube (e.g. in an ice bath):**","The forward reaction is endothermic ($\\Delta H > 0$), so the reverse reaction is exothermic. Lowering the temperature makes the system partly counteract the change by favouring the exothermic (reverse) direction, which releases heat. The equilibrium shifts left and the colour changes towards pink. Cooling also lowers the value of $K_{eq}$.","","**Change 2 — Remove chloride ions (add a little silver nitrate solution):**","$\\mathrm{Ag^+}$ removes $\\mathrm{Cl^-}$ as a white precipitate of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$. With less $\\mathrm{Cl^-}$ in the denominator,","$$Q = \\frac{[\\mathrm{CoCl_4^{2-}}]}{[\\mathrm{Co(H_2O)_6^{2+}}][\\mathrm{Cl^-}]^4}$$","becomes larger than $K_{eq}$. The system shifts left to restore equilibrium, forming more pink $\\mathrm{[Co(H_2O)_6]^{2+}}$.","","(Adding a large volume of water also works: dilution lowers every concentration, and because there are 5 dissolved particles on the left but only 1 on the right, $Q$ rises above $K_{eq}$ and the system shifts left.)","","Adding a catalyst would not work, because it speeds up both directions equally and does not move the position of equilibrium."].join(`
`),marks:3}}},wZe=["CH-M6-2"],vZe=["Neutralisation of weak bases with strong acids","Writing balanced neutralisation equations","Predicting the acidity of salt solutions","Hydrolysis of conjugate acids of weak bases"],xZe={id:"chemistry/2021/q23",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...wZe],learningTopics:vZe,generate(t){return{question:"Fertiliser-grade ammonium nitrate can be made by passing ammonia gas into dilute nitric acid until the acid is exactly neutralised.",parts:[{question:"Write a balanced equation, with states, for the formation of ammonium nitrate from aqueous ammonia and nitric acid.",sampleAnswer:["$$\\mathrm{NH_3(aq) + HNO_3(aq) \\rightarrow NH_4NO_3(aq)}$$","","Nitric acid donates a proton to ammonia, forming the ammonium ion. Unlike the reaction of an acid with a hydroxide, no water is formed, because ammonia accepts $\\mathrm{H^+}$ directly rather than supplying $\\mathrm{OH^-}$."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:'A student claims that because the acid was "exactly neutralised", the ammonium nitrate solution has a pH of 7. Evaluate this claim.',sampleAnswer:["The claim is **incorrect**: the ammonium nitrate solution is slightly **acidic** (pH below 7).","",'"Exactly neutralised" only means that equal amounts of acid and base have reacted. The pH of the resulting salt solution depends on how its ions react with water.',"","- $\\mathrm{NO_3^-}$ is the conjugate base of a **strong** acid. It has negligible tendency to accept protons, so it does not affect the pH.","- $\\mathrm{NH_4^+}$ is the conjugate acid of a **weak** base, ammonia. It donates some protons to water:","$$\\mathrm{NH_4^+(aq) + H_2O(l) \\rightleftharpoons NH_3(aq) + H_3O^+(aq)}$$","","This reaction produces $\\mathrm{H_3O^+}$, so $[\\mathrm{H_3O^+}] > [\\mathrm{OH^-}]$ and the pH is below 7. A pH of exactly 7 at the equivalence point is expected only when a strong acid reacts with a strong base."].join(`
