`),parts:[{question:"Describe a chemical test that could be done in a school laboratory to confirm that Q is an alkene. Include expected observations in your answer.",sampleAnswer:["**Test:** Add a few drops of **bromine water** to a sample of Q in a test tube.","**Observation:** The bromine water is **decolourised** (changes from orange/brown to colourless) due to the addition of $\\mathrm{Br_2}$ across the carbon–carbon double bond.","$$\\mathrm{C=C + Br_2 \\rightarrow C(Br)\\!-\\!C(Br)}$$","","Alternatively, acidified potassium permanganate ($\\mathrm{KMnO_4}$) could be added; it would be decolourised from purple to colourless."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:"Compound R contains 31.9% carbon, 5.3% hydrogen and 62.8% chlorine by mass. Mass spectrometry shows that the molar mass of R is approximately $113\\,\\mathrm{g\\,mol^{-1}}$. Provide a structural formula for compound R. Support your answer with calculations.",sampleAnswer:["The question tests finding an empirical formula from percentage composition, using the molar mass to get the molecular formula, and then linking it to an alkene addition reaction.","","**Step 1: empirical formula.** Assume $100\\,\\mathrm{g}$ of R, so the percentages become masses in grams, then convert each to moles.","$$n(\\mathrm{C}) = \\frac{31.9}{12.01} = 2.656\\,\\mathrm{mol}$$","$$n(\\mathrm{H}) = \\frac{5.3}{1.008} = 5.258\\,\\mathrm{mol}$$","$$n(\\mathrm{Cl}) = \\frac{62.8}{35.45} = 1.772\\,\\mathrm{mol}$$","Divide each by the smallest value ($1.772$):","$$\\mathrm{C} : \\mathrm{H} : \\mathrm{Cl} = 1.50 : 2.97 : 1$$","The ratio for C is not close to a whole number, so multiply every value by 2:","$$\\mathrm{C} : \\mathrm{H} : \\mathrm{Cl} = 3.00 : 5.93 : 2 \\approx 3 : 6 : 2$$","The empirical formula is $\\mathrm{C_3H_6Cl_2}$. A common trap is to round $1.50$ to $2$; a value ending in $.5$ means the whole ratio must be doubled.","","**Step 2: molecular formula.**","$$M(\\mathrm{C_3H_6Cl_2}) = 3(12.01) + 6(1.008) + 2(35.45) = 113.0\\,\\mathrm{g\\,mol^{-1}}$$","This equals the measured molar mass, so the molecular formula of R is also $\\mathrm{C_3H_6Cl_2}$.","","**Step 3: structure.** R forms when $\\mathrm{Cl_2}$ adds across a C=C double bond, so Q is $\\mathrm{C_3H_6}$ (propene), and one Cl atom ends up on each of the two carbons that formed the double bond:","$$\\mathrm{CH_2{=}CHCH_3 + Cl_2 \\rightarrow CH_2ClCHClCH_3}$$","Other isomers of $\\mathrm{C_3H_6Cl_2}$ (such as 1,1- or 1,3-dichloropropane) cannot be made by adding $\\mathrm{Cl_2}$ to an alkene.","","Compound R is **1,2-dichloropropane**, $\\mathrm{CH_2ClCHClCH_3}$."].join(`
`),marks:3}]}}},Bit=["CH-M7-4"],zit=["Solubility of alcohols in water","Hydrogen bonding between alcohols and water","Dispersion forces and hydrocarbon chain length","Trends in properties of a homologous series"],Dit={id:"chemistry/2023/q29",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Bit],learningTopics:zit,generate(t){return{question:["A chemist measured how much of several straight-chain alcohols could dissolve in water at about 20°C. The results are in the table.","","| Alcohol | Carbon atoms | Solubility (g per 100 g water) |","| --- | ---: | ---: |","| Propan-1-ol | 3 | fully miscible |","| Butan-1-ol | 4 | 7.4 |","| Pentan-1-ol | 5 | 2.2 |","| Hexan-1-ol | 6 | 0.59 |","| Heptan-1-ol | 7 | 0.17 |","","Account for the pattern in these results in terms of the intermolecular forces involved."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Pattern:** as the carbon chain gets longer, the alcohols become much less soluble in water. Propan-1-ol mixes in any proportion, but each extra $\\mathrm{CH_2}$ after that cuts the solubility to less than a third of the previous value.","","**Why alcohols dissolve at all.** Every molecule in the table has one hydroxyl group ($\\mathrm{-OH}$). This polar group can form **hydrogen bonds** with water molecules, and these new alcohol–water hydrogen bonds help to make up for the water–water hydrogen bonds that must be broken for the alcohol to fit in.","","**Why solubility falls.** The rest of each molecule is a non-polar hydrocarbon chain, which interacts with its neighbours only through **dispersion forces**. It cannot hydrogen bond with water, so it breaks up the water hydrogen-bond network without replacing it. As the chain lengthens:","- the non-polar part of the molecule becomes a larger share of the molecule, while there is still only one $\\mathrm{-OH}$ group;","- the dispersion forces between alcohol molecules become stronger (more electrons), so the alcohol molecules attract each other more than they attract water.","","Dissolving therefore becomes less and less favourable, and the solubility drops sharply.","","A common error is to say that longer alcohols form weaker hydrogen bonds. Each molecule still has one $\\mathrm{-OH}$ group; what changes is the growing non-polar chain and its dispersion forces."].join(`
`),marks:3}}},Wit=["CH-M8-1"],Uit=["Qualitative tests for anions","Test for sulfate ions with barium ions","Test for halide ions with silver nitrate","Sequencing tests to avoid interfering precipitates","Writing net ionic equations"],Git={id:"chemistry/2023/q30",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Wit],learningTopics:Uit,generate(t){return{question:["A technician finds an unlabelled bottle of colourless solution. Stock records show that it contains sulfate ions ($\\mathrm{SO_4^{2-}}$), chloride ions ($\\mathrm{Cl^-}$), or both, each at about $0.5\\,\\mathrm{mol\\,L^{-1}}$.","","Describe a series of school laboratory tests that would show which of these anions are in the solution. State the expected observations and give TWO balanced net ionic equations."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["The key is the **order** of the tests. Silver sulfate is only slightly soluble, so at these concentrations adding silver ions first could give a white precipitate even if no chloride were present. Remove any sulfate first, then test for chloride.","","**Test 1 – sulfate.** Acidify a sample with dilute nitric acid (this removes any carbonate or hydroxide that could also precipitate), then add barium nitrate solution drop by drop until no more change occurs.","- A **white precipitate** of barium sulfate means sulfate is present:","$$\\mathrm{Ba^{2+}(aq) + SO_4^{2-}(aq) \\rightarrow BaSO_4(s)}$$","- No precipitate means sulfate is absent.","- Nitrate salts are used (not barium chloride or hydrochloric acid) so that no chloride is added to the sample.","","**Test 2 – chloride.** Filter off any barium sulfate, then add silver nitrate solution to the filtrate.","- A **white precipitate** of silver chloride (which darkens in sunlight) means chloride is present:","$$\\mathrm{Ag^+(aq) + Cl^-(aq) \\rightarrow AgCl(s)}$$","- No precipitate means chloride is absent.","","Together the two results show whether the bottle holds sulfate only, chloride only, or both."].join(`
