`),choices:i,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["A market-based environmental policy puts a price on pollution and then lets firms decide how much to cut. Direct regulation instead tells firms exactly what they may or may not do.","","A capped number of tradeable permits is an emissions trading scheme. The cap fixes total emissions, and trading creates a market price for each tonne emitted. Firms that can cut emissions cheaply do so and sell spare permits, while firms facing high costs buy them. The price signal drives the reduction, so this is a market-based policy.","","A ban on new power stations and a rule that every factory must fit the same filter are direct regulations: they set rules rather than using prices. A sales tax on electric vehicles does use a price signal, but it makes the low-emission choice dearer, so it would tend to raise emissions rather than cut them."].join(`
