`),sampleAnswer:a,marks:25}}},D9t=[z9t],W9t=["EX1-EL-1","EX1-EL-2","EX1-EL-3","EX1-EL-4","EX1-EL-5"],U9t=["understanding how texts challenge dominant assumptions","analysing prescribed texts in depth","synthesizing ideas across multiple texts",'exploring how texts "break" conventional narratives',"constructing sophisticated comparative arguments","integrating related texts to extend analysis"],sG=[{elective:"Literary Homelands",stimulusAuthor:"Sample Author",stimulusText:['Every homeland is a story told and retold, a narrative constructed from memory, longing, and the selective forgetting that makes belonging possible. Literary representations of homelands rarely present these spaces as static or uncomplicated; instead, they reveal how the idea of "home" is perpetually contested, reimagined, and broken apart by those who inherit it.',"","The most powerful homeland narratives are those that refuse easy nostalgia, that show how dominant stories of place exclude, erase, or silence alternative experiences. In breaking these dominant narratives, literature opens space for voices that have been marginalized or suppressed."].join(`
`),prompt:"How do the texts you have studied creatively break dominant assumptions about homelands? In your response, make close reference to TWO prescribed texts from the Literary Homelands elective and ONE other text of your own choosing.",sampleAnswer:["This is a template generator for the Electives section. In practice, each specific elective and prescribed text combination would have its own detailed sample answer demonstrating how to structure a response that:","","- Engages directly with the stimulus text's ideas","- Analyzes TWO prescribed texts in depth, showing how each text challenges dominant narratives or assumptions","- Integrates ONE related text of own choosing to extend the argument","- Synthesizes across all three texts to build a cohesive critical perspective",'- Demonstrates sophisticated understanding of how texts use form, structure, and language to "break" conventional representations',"","Extension 1 responses are expected to be highly analytical, integrating critical vocabulary and demonstrating metacognitive awareness of how texts construct meaning and challenge assumptions.","","A complete response would be 900-1200 words, organized in clear paragraphs that build a sustained argument connecting the stimulus text to the student's chosen texts."].join(`
`)},{elective:"Worlds of Upheaval",stimulusAuthor:"Sample Author",stimulusText:["Texts about upheaval do not simply document disruption; they perform it. They break narrative conventions, fragment chronology, destabilize perspective, and refuse resolution — all to make readers experience rather than merely observe the disintegration of social, political, or personal order.","","In doing so, these texts challenge the dominant assumption that stories should provide coherence, clarity, and closure. They insist instead that some experiences resist neat narrative containment."].join(`
`),prompt:"How do the texts you have studied creatively break dominant assumptions about representing upheaval? In your response, make close reference to TWO prescribed texts from the Worlds of Upheaval elective and ONE other text of your own choosing.",sampleAnswer:["Template response for Worlds of Upheaval elective. A complete answer would analyze how prescribed texts from this elective use formal innovation and narrative disruption to challenge conventional representations of social or personal upheaval."].join(`
`)},{elective:"Reimagined Worlds",stimulusAuthor:"Sample Author",stimulusText:['To reimagine a world is not simply to invent an alternative reality, but to expose the assumptions underpinning the world as we know it. By constructing worlds that operate under different rules, speculative and fantastical texts reveal that the "natural" order of things is in fact contingent, constructed, and open to radical transformation.',"","The power of reimagined worlds lies in their capacity to make the familiar strange, showing readers that what we take for granted is merely one possibility among many."].join(`
`),prompt:"How do the texts you have studied creatively break dominant assumptions through reimagining worlds? In your response, make close reference to TWO prescribed texts from the Reimagined Worlds elective and ONE other text of your own choosing.",sampleAnswer:["Template response for Reimagined Worlds elective. A complete answer would examine how texts in this elective use speculative, fantastical, or alternative-reality narratives to challenge assumptions about social structures, identity, or reality itself."].join(`
`)},{elective:"Literary Mindscapes",stimulusAuthor:"Sample Author",stimulusText:['Texts that explore interior consciousness challenge the dominant assumption that reality is primarily external and objective. By foregrounding subjective experience, memory, and psychological complexity, these texts assert that inner worlds are as "real" and as worthy of serious representation as the social or physical world.',"","In breaking conventional narrative forms to represent consciousness, these texts also break the assumption that stories should be linear, coherent, and easily comprehensible."].join(`
`),prompt:"How do the texts you have studied creatively break dominant assumptions about representing consciousness? In your response, make close reference to TWO prescribed texts from the Literary Mindscapes elective and ONE other text of your own choosing.",sampleAnswer:["Template response for Literary Mindscapes elective. A complete answer would analyze how prescribed texts use stream-of-consciousness, fragmented narration, or other formal innovations to represent interior experience in ways that challenge conventional realist assumptions."].join(`
`)},{elective:"Intersecting Worlds",stimulusAuthor:"Sample Author",stimulusText:["When different worlds — natural and human, past and present, indigenous and settler — intersect in literature, the result is often not harmonious synthesis but productive tension. These texts reveal that different ways of knowing and being in the world cannot always be reconciled into a single coherent narrative.","","By refusing to resolve these tensions, literature challenges the dominant assumption that there is one true or superior way of understanding reality."].join(`
`),prompt:"How do the texts you have studied creatively break dominant assumptions about the relationship between different worlds? In your response, make close reference to TWO prescribed texts from the Intersecting Worlds elective and ONE other text of your own choosing.",sampleAnswer:["Template response for Intersecting Worlds elective. A complete answer would explore how texts represent the collision or coexistence of different worldviews, knowledge systems, or ways of being, and how this challenges assumptions about universal truths or singular perspectives."].join(`
