`),choices:a,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["The rod is light and the pivot is frictionless, so the torque about O comes from the","weight of the bob. The pivot force acts through O, so it has no torque about O.","","Torque is force times the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the line of","action of the force. With the rod at angle $\\theta$ to the vertical:","","$$\\tau = r \\, mg \\sin\\theta$$","","- **At B**, the rod hangs straight down, so $\\theta = 0$. The weight acts along","  the line through O, the perpendicular distance is zero, and $\\tau = 0$.","- **At A and C**, the rod makes its largest angle with the vertical, so","  $\\sin\\theta$ and the torque are **largest**.","","The torque always points back towards B. It is a restoring torque, so it swaps","direction as the bob passes through B. Its size also changes continuously with","$\\theta$, so options that describe a constant size are wrong. The option with the","largest torque at B mixes up torque with speed: the bob moves fastest at B, but the","torque there is zero."].join(`
