`),sampleAnswer:["**Laboratory frame: time dilation.** In the laboratory the pions move at","nearly the speed of light. Their half-life is a *proper time*, measured by a","clock that moves with them. The laboratory observers see this moving clock","run slow ($t = t_0/\\sqrt{1 - v^2/c^2}$). At $0.99c$ the half-life measured","in the laboratory is several times longer than 18 ns. The 60 m trip then","lasts only about one and a half of these longer half-lives, instead of more","than ten, so a sizeable fraction of the pions survives.","","**Pions' frame: length contraction.** In their own frame the pions are at rest","and have their normal half-life of 18 ns. Now the tube and the detector rush","towards them at $0.99c$. The 60 m length is a *proper length*, measured in the","laboratory where the tube is at rest. The pions measure a **contracted**","length ($L = L_0\\sqrt{1 - v^2/c^2}$), much shorter than 60 m. The detector","reaches them after only about one and a half half-lives, so a sizeable","fraction of them has not yet decayed.","","**Consistency.** Both frames predict the same observable result: the same","number of pions reaches the detector. They explain it in different ways. Time","dilation in one frame and length contraction in the other are two views of","the same effect, since the speed of light is the same in every inertial frame.","The same reasoning explains why muons made by cosmic rays high in the","atmosphere are detected at Earth's surface."].join(`
