`)}}}},v0a=["SE-PF-1","SE-PROJ-2"],x0a=["Waterfall software development approach","Agile software development approach","WAgile (hybrid) software development approach","sprints and iterative development","sequential stages and sign-off in Waterfall","choosing a development approach to suit project requirements"],NX=["Waterfall","Agile","WAgile"],Rb=["each stage is completed and signed off before the next stage begins","working software is delivered in short iterations and requirements may change","a fixed up-front plan and budget is combined with short development iterations"],HX=[{scenario:"A start-up is building a fitness app. The client keeps suggesting new features after seeing each fortnightly demo, and the team adjusts its to-do list every two weeks.",answer:1,explanation:["The clues are **fortnightly demos** and **changing requirements**. The team works in short","cycles (sprints), shows working software to the client and re-plans after each one. That is","Agile, whose defining feature is iterative delivery with requirements that are allowed to change."]},{scenario:"A government department is replacing a payroll calculator. Every requirement is fixed by legislation, the full specification must be approved before any coding starts, and testing only begins after coding is complete.",answer:0,explanation:["The clues are **fixed requirements**, a specification that is **approved before coding**,","and testing that happens **after** coding. Each stage is finished and signed off before the","next starts, which is the Waterfall approach. Waterfall suits projects like this where","requirements are stable and well understood at the start."]},{scenario:"A bank must agree on a fixed budget and delivery date with its board before a project starts, but the developers then build and review the new mobile features in two-week sprints.",answer:2,explanation:["This project mixes the two approaches. The **up-front plan, budget and deadline** come from","Waterfall, while the **two-week sprints** come from Agile. A hybrid of the two is called","WAgile. It is common in organisations that need fixed approvals but still want the","flexibility of iterative development."]},{scenario:"A school is commissioning a new canteen ordering website. The developer must collect and document all requirements, then design, then code, then test, and the school will not see the website until it is finished.",answer:0,explanation:["Each stage (requirements, design, coding, testing) happens **once, in order**, and the client","sees nothing until the end. That is the Waterfall approach. The risk is that if a","requirement was misunderstood, it is only discovered late, when it is expensive to fix."]}],k0a={id:"software_engineering/2025/q02",version:1,variants:HX.length,syllabusTopics:[...v0a],learningTopics:x0a,generate(t){const e=HX[t];return{kind:"dropdown",question:[e.scenario,"","Complete the sentence by selecting the correct options.","","This project is using the {{0}} approach, because {{1}}."].join(`
